{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be taking care of you tonight. Can you tell me what brought you to the hospital?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Hi, Doctor. I’ve been having trouble breathing and chest tightness all day. It started when I was cleaning the house—vacuuming and dusting. I thought it was just another asthma flare-up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. You were seen in the ER earlier and got nebulizers and a steroid shot. Your oxygen levels were good, and you didn’t need oxygen. But since this is the fourth time in three weeks, we want to figure out what’s really going on."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It’s scary. I feel like I can’t get air in, but I don’t wheeze like before. The inhalers help a little, but not like they used to."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s important. On exam, your lungs don’t have wheezing, and your peak flow was better than expected for a severe asthma attack. That’s why we’re thinking it might not be your lungs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Then what could it be?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We consulted pulmonary, and they suspect vocal cord dysfunction. That means your vocal cords might be closing when they shouldn’t, especially when you’re stressed or active. It mimics asthma but doesn’t respond well to inhalers."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh… I did notice my voice sounded different when I was struggling. But I’ve always been treated for asthma."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. And you’ve been on steroids multiple times recently, which we want to avoid long-term. We’ll keep you on a short taper this time, but the goal is to find other ways to manage this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. What happens now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll monitor you overnight. You’ll have pulmonary function tests tomorrow morning to help confirm this. ENT will see you as an outpatient for a scope of your vocal cords."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will the inhalers still help at all?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They might give a little relief, but the main thing will be learning techniques to relax your breathing. Your psychiatrist can help with that, since stress can trigger this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That makes sense. I’ve been anxious lately."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:29:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll keep your lorazepam available for anxiety. Also, your blood sugar has been high—around the 200s and 300s—because of the steroids. We’re using insulin to manage that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Right, I’m on glyburide for diabetes. Will I go back to that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2140-01-05 20:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, once the steroids are out of your system, we’ll restart glyburide. Your sugars should come down then."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How are you feeling today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better. I haven’t needed the nebulizers since last night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s great. Your labs this morning look stable. No infection, and your heart enzymes were normal, so we’re not worried about a heart issue."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the breathing test?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The pulmonary function test showed poor airflow, but not the pattern we see in asthma. And bronchodilators didn’t improve it much. That supports vocal cord dysfunction."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my lungs?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not primarily. Your lungs are clear on the chest X-ray—no pneumonia or fluid. The problem seems to be in your upper airway, not deep in the lungs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What do I do when it happens again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Try your albuterol inhaler first, just in case there’s some asthma component. Then focus on slow, calm breathing. Try to stay relaxed. Panic can make it worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Like breathing through pursed lips?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, or diaphragmatic breathing. Your psychiatrist can help you practice techniques. Also, avoid triggers like dust or strong cleaners."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll try. What about my other meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll go home on all your usual medications—amlodipine, simvastatin, omeprazole, your inhalers, and the steroid taper we discussed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "The prednisone taper?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:29:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. Start with three tablets a day for three days, then two, then one, then half. It’s important to taper slowly to avoid side effects."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. And my propranolol for my headaches and pressure in my head?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2140-01-06 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, continue that. But your PCP should review it, because it can sometimes affect breathing if there’s a lung component. Your pulmonologist will help sort that out."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2140-01-06 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. We’re planning to discharge you tomorrow morning if everything stays stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2140-01-06 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. I feel much better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2140-01-06 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll follow up with the pulmonary clinic in a few days. They’ll help confirm the vocal cord issue and guide next steps."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2140-01-06 14:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if I get short of breath again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2140-01-06 14:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Try your inhaler and relaxation first. If you’re very worried or it doesn’t improve, go to the ER—preferably here, so they have your records."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2140-01-07 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re cleared to go home. Your breathing is stable, and labs are good. Here’s your discharge summary and prescriptions."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2140-01-07 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. So the main diagnosis is vocal cord dysfunction?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2140-01-07 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s the most likely cause. But we’re not ruling out some asthma or COPD, so keep using your inhalers as prescribed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2140-01-07 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And no more frequent steroid bursts?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2140-01-07 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We want to avoid that. Focus on breathing techniques and avoiding triggers. Your care team will help you manage this long-term."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2140-01-07 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ll make sure to keep my follow-up appointments."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2140-01-07 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good plan. Take care, and call your doctor if anything changes."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2140-01-05 20:04:00",
    "admission_end": "2140-01-07 16:45:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of type 2 diabetes, hypertension, GERD, pseudotumor cerebri, and multiple recent hospitalizations for dyspnea was admitted for shortness of breath and chest tightness after cleaning her home. Initial concern for asthma exacerbation was reconsidered due to lack of wheezing, suboptimal response to bronchodilators, and normal chest imaging. Pulmonary function tests showed poor airflow without obstructive pattern, leading to a diagnosis of vocal cord dysfunction. She improved with supportive care and a short steroid taper. Chronic conditions were managed, including insulin for steroid-induced hyperglycemia. She was discharged home with a prednisone taper, continued home medications, and follow-up planned with pulmonary and psychiatry for breathing techniques and vocal cord evaluation.",
    "problems": [
      "Other diseases of vocal cords",
      "Chronic obstructive asthma, unspecified",
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Obstructive sleep apnea (adult)(pediatric)",
      "Benign intracranial hypertension",
      "Dysthymic disorder",
      "Pain in joint, shoulder region",
      "Hyperaldosteronism, unspecified",
      "Migraine, unspecified, without mention of intractable migraine without mention of status migrainosus"
    ]
  }
}
